[{"expectations":"Every target-language word and sentence is grammatical, natural and in the requested variant (US English, Brazilian Portuguese or Mexican Spanish, including vocabulary like \"departamento\" and \"renta\" in Mexico). Every translation has exactly the meaning of the original. Tips, notes and explanations say only true things about both languages. A wrong translation, an ungrammatical sentence or a false rule teaches the learner a mistake; the wrong variant is a smaller cost (they'd sound out of place).","id":"accuracy","label":"Accuracy"},{"expectations":"The lesson is written for speakers of the learner's language: pronunciation tips name the sound those speakers get wrong and how to fix it, notes catch real false friends, sentence explanations compare with how the learner would say it, and respellings use only letters those speakers read naturally, with syllables and the stressed one in capitals. Respellings never add a sound the word doesn't have: for Brazilian readers, no vowel before an initial \"r\" or an \"s\" + consonant (\"RÉNT\", \"SKUL\", never \"a-RÉNT\" or \"is-CUL\") and none after a final consonant (\"BIG\", never \"BI-gui\"), since that teaches the very mistake Brazilians make; an English \"r\" is a single \"r\" with a tip on making it, never \"rr\", which reads as \"h\". A respelling that adds a syllable teaches the learner to say the word wrong. Generic tips that would fit any learner, invented problems and IPA symbols don't help this learner. Null tips and notes are right when there is nothing real to say.","id":"pairAwareness","label":"Written for this language pair"},{"expectations":"Words and sentences serve the lesson's exact situation and can-do, at the CEFR level given: at A1 and A2 short everyday sentences, more at B1 and B2. Nothing from the known words list is taught again. Words are the ones a learner in that situation needs first, not a padded list of variants.","id":"situationAndLevel","label":"Situation and level"},{"expectations":"Wrong options are plausible for a learner yet clearly wrong where they're used, and never appear in the answer next to them. The tip names one real pattern the sentences use, and its examples and practice all test that pattern. Each practice blank has exactly one right answer. Writing answers are all correct and cover common right variants. Speaking prompts set up a real moment in the learner's language. When the situation is a conversation the learner takes part in, a dialogue ends on the other person's line and has exactly one best reply, and every other reply is wrong there for a reason a learner would fall for; a lesson like that without a dialogue scores at most 7. A pattern table appears only when the tip is about a set of endings: the new word follows the model in every row, and each blank's right ending is the longest choice its answer ends with.","id":"practiceDesign","label":"Practice design"},{"expectations":"Text in the learner's language is natural, concise and correct in its variant. Target-language fields (words, sentences, templates, answers) contain no learner-language words; learner-language fields may quote target-language words in quotes or backticks when they talk about them, which is intended. Target sentences and their translations, which become word tiles, have no decorative final periods or exclamation marks, while questions keep their question marks; other fields use normal punctuation. Summary lines each state one thing the learner can now say.","id":"style","label":"Language and style"},{"expectations":"A person with no background in the subject follows it on the first read: everyday words, short sentences, and examples from everyday life they've seen or done, with a technical term only after the plain idea it names. Ask whether a grandmother would understand it. An output that is accurate but reads academic, abstract or dull fails its reader as surely as a wrong fact does: they stop reading or don't take it in. Notice jargon left unexplained, definitions before any example, abstract nouns where a concrete case fits, generic examples (\"a company\", \"a person\"), and a tone that reads like a textbook. A term the reader already knows for their goal is fine.","id":"plainLanguage","label":"Easy to follow"}]